/// Move, rotate and scale a shape and keep its bounding box, as in
/// `transform.translate(sphere(1), [2, 0, 0])`.

// =========================================================
// SHAPE TRANSFORMS
// =========================================================
// Move a shape and keep its bounding box. A remap moves a shape too, but it
// clears .box: an arbitrary coordinate says nothing about where the shape
// went. Each function here says exactly where, so .box follows the shape.
//
// Each takes the shape as a value — transform.translate(sphere(1), [2, 0, 0])
// — and returns the moved shape. Rotations turn the shape counterclockwise,
// looking down the axis toward the origin, the same sense as map.rotateXY and
// friends applied to the coordinate.
//
// Each is one remap whose coordinate reads only p, its own parameters and
// builtins, written out rather than calling map's rotations, so the shape's
// colour and material move with it (property's unwrapRemaps): a call to
// another package there would name an import its caller may not have.

/// The shape moved by `offset`.
translate(shape, offset) {
  return remap(shape, p - offset);
}

/// The shape turned by `angle` radians about the x axis.
rotateX(shape, angle) {
  return remap(shape, [p.x, p.y * cos(angle) + p.z * sin(angle), -p.y * sin(angle) + p.z * cos(angle)]);
}

/// The shape turned by `angle` radians about the y axis.
rotateY(shape, angle) {
  return remap(shape, [p.x * cos(angle) - p.z * sin(angle), p.y, p.z * cos(angle) + p.x * sin(angle)]);
}

/// The shape turned by `angle` radians about the z axis.
rotateZ(shape, angle) {
  return remap(shape, [p.x * cos(angle) + p.y * sin(angle), -p.x * sin(angle) + p.y * cos(angle), p.z]);
}

/// The shape turned by `angle` radians about the line through `origin` along
/// `direction`, which need not be normalized: Rodrigues' formula turning the
/// point back by `angle`, as map.rotateAxis(p, origin, direction, -angle).
rotate(shape, origin, direction, angle) {
  return remap(shape, origin + (p - origin) * cos(angle) - cross(normalize(direction), p - origin) * sin(angle) + normalize(direction) * dot(normalize(direction), p - origin) * (1 - cos(angle)));
}

/// The shape scaled by `factor` about the origin. The factor must be positive;
/// the distance scales with the shape, so the field stays a distance.
scale(shape, factor) {
  return remap(shape, p / factor) * factor;
}

/// The shape carried by the 4×4 affine matrix `m` — build it with
/// matrix.translation, matrix.rotation, matrix.scaling and matrix.compose. The
/// distance is scaled by m's smallest scale, which keeps it a bound when m
/// stretches unevenly.
affine(shape, m) {
  return remap(shape, vector.multiplyPoint(matrix.inverse(m), p)) * matrix.minScale(m);
}
